"{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1353/lan.2015.0055\",\n  \"title\": \"On statistical measures and ancient writing systems\",\n  \"authors\": \"Rajesh P. N. Rao; Rob Lee; Nisha Yadav; Mayank Vahia; Philip Jonathan; Pauline Ziman\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>\\n                    An article published in\\n                    <jats:italic>Language</jats:italic>\\n                    (Sproat 2014a) questions our findings on the Indus script and Pictish symbols published in the journals\\n                    <jats:italic>Science</jats:italic>\\n                    (Rao et al. 2009a),\\n                    <jats:italic>PNAS</jats:italic>\\n                    (Rao et al. 2009b),\\n                    <jats:italic>IEEE Computer</jats:italic>\\n                    (Rao 2010), and the\\n                    <jats:italic>Proceedings of the Royal Society</jats:italic>\\n                    (Lee et al. 2010a,b). Sproat's article does not accurately present our methods and findings, and its conclusions are based on what appears to be a misunderstanding of our proposed approach. For example, the article's results on entropic measures seem to favor, rather than contradict, the inductive hypothesis that the Indus script may represent writing. The article selects results to draw a particular set of conclusions and convey a specific viewpoint. In light of these issues, we stand by our original findings.\\n                  </jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1353/lan.2015.0055\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2015-12-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1353/lan.2015.0055\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 3,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Cambridge University Press (CUP)', 'container_title': 'Language', 'volume': '91', 'issue': '4', 'page': 'e198-e205', 'issn': ['0097-8507', '1535-0665'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '56', 'prefix': '10.1017'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1163/2214-448x_eagll_eagllcom_00000170\",\n  \"title\": \"Greek Writing Systems\",\n  \"authors\": \"\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1163/2214-448x_eagll_eagllcom_00000170\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1970-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1163/2214-448x_eagll_eagllcom_00000170\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"dataset\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Walter de Gruyter GmbH', 'container_title': 'Encyclopedia of Ancient Greek Language and Linguistics', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'dataset', 'member': '374', 'prefix': '10.1163'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1163/2214-448x_eagll_com_00000170\",\n  \"title\": \"Greek Writing Systems\",\n  \"authors\": \"\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1163/2214-448x_eagll_com_00000170\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1970-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1163/2214-448x_eagll_com_00000170\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"dataset\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Walter de Gruyter GmbH', 'container_title': 'Encyclopedia of Ancient Greek Language and Linguistics', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'dataset', 'member': '374', 'prefix': '10.1163'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1017/cbo9781139567701.007\",\n  \"title\": \"Ancient writing in Mesopotamia\",\n  \"authors\": \"\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1017/cbo9781139567701.007\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2015-05-28T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9781139567701.007\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 2,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Cambridge University Press', 'container_title': 'Language, Literacy, and Technology', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '135-149', 'issn': [], 'isbn': ['9781139567701', '9781107036482', '9781107642850'], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '56', 'prefix': '10.1017'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2307/jj.21275882.34\",\n  \"title\": \"Adjustment of the Ancient Egyptian Writing System as a Reflection of Language Change\",\n  \"authors\": \"FRANK KAMMERZELL\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.2307/jj.21275882.34\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2023-12-05T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2307/jj.21275882.34\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'IFAO', 'container_title': 'Guide to the Writing Systems of Ancient Egypt', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '182-187', 'issn': [], 'isbn': ['9782724710342', '9782724708738'], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '1121', 'prefix': '10.2307'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1016/j.jslw.2015.02.002\",\n  \"title\": \"Different topics, different discourse: Relationships among writing topic, measures of syntactic complexity, and judgments of writing quality\",\n  \"authors\": \"Weiwei Yang; Xiaofei Lu; Sara Cushing Weigle\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1016/j.jslw.2015.02.002\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2015-06-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jslw.2015.02.002\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 244,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Elsevier BV', 'container_title': 'Journal of Second Language Writing', 'volume': '28', 'issue': '', 'page': '53-67', 'issn': ['1060-3743'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '78', 'prefix': '10.1016'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1016/j.jslw.2025.101226\",\n  \"title\": \"Discourse complexity measures as indices of L2 writing proficiency: Insights from propositional connection and relation\",\n  \"authors\": \"Jingting Yuan; Qiuhan Lin; John Sie Yuen Lee\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1016/j.jslw.2025.101226\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2025-09-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jslw.2025.101226\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 2,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Elsevier BV', 'container_title': 'Journal of Second Language Writing', 'volume': '69', 'issue': '', 'page': '101226', 'issn': ['1060-3743'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '78', 'prefix': '10.1016'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1177/25138502241273899\",\n  \"title\": \"Reflecting the language of ancient civilizations: A diachronic transition of “爵” and contrastive analysis of  Sino-Korean Chinese characters\",\n  \"authors\": \"Sunhee Lee; Sungduk Cho\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>This study focuses on the historical and cultural significance of the Chinese character “爵” (jue), reflecting the culture of the ancient Bronze Age, and examines its semantic evolution and characteristics from a cognitive linguistic perspective. In addition, it compares and contrasts this with its use in Korean hanja (Chinese characters used in the Korean language). The research highlights that in ancient China, “爵” was initially used to denote a ceremonial wine vessel, symbolizing authority and social status, and was closely associated with rites and nobility. Over time, the meanings of “爵” expanded to include titles of nobility and official positions. Furthermore, the study explores the impact of Chinese culture and language on Korea, showing how “爵” and its related concepts were adopted and adapted within the Korean language and culture, maintaining some of its original meanings while also developing unique interpretations. It suggests that many Korean hanja words share identical meanings with their Chinese counterparts, but some have evolved distinctively, especially in the realm of religion, highlighting the interplay and diversity of cultural and linguistic influences between China and Korea.</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1177/25138502241273899\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2024-09-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/pdf/10.1177/25138502241273899\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1177/25138502241273899\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'SAGE Publications', 'container_title': 'Journal of Chinese Writing Systems', 'volume': '8', 'issue': '3', 'page': '191-200', 'issn': ['2513-8502', '2513-8510'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '179', 'prefix': '10.1177'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1111/lang.12119\",\n  \"title\": \"Statistical Measures for Usage‐Based Linguistics\",\n  \"authors\": \"Stefan Th. Gries; Nick C. Ellis\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>The advent of usage‐/exemplar‐based approaches has resulted in a major change in the theoretical landscape of linguistics, but also in the range of methodologies that are brought to bear on the study of</jats:p><jats:p>language acquisition/learning, structure, and use. In particular, methods from corpus linguistics are now frequently used to study distributional characteristics of linguistics units and what they reveal about cognitive and psycholinguistic processes. This paper surveys a range of psycholinguistic notions that are becoming ever more important in theoretical and cognitive linguistics—for example, frequency, entrenchment, dispersion, contingency, surprisal, Zipfian distributions—and current corpus‐linguistic approaches toward exploring these notions and their roles for linguistic cognition.</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1111/lang.12119\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2015-05-21T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"https://api.wiley.com/onlinelibrary/tdm/v1/articles/10.1111%2Flang.12119\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1111/lang.12119\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 153,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Wiley', 'container_title': 'Language Learning', 'volume': '65', 'issue': 'S1', 'page': '228-255', 'issn': ['0023-8333', '1467-9922'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '311', 'prefix': '10.1111'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1075/wll.12.2.01lee\",\n  \"title\": \"Writing systems and linguistic structure\",\n  \"authors\": \"Sang-Oak Lee\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1075/wll.12.2.01lee\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2009-12-15T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1075/wll.12.2.01lee\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'John Benjamins Publishing Company', 'container_title': 'Written Language &amp; Literacy', 'volume': '12', 'issue': '2', 'page': '159-160', 'issn': ['1387-6732', '1570-6001'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '1757', 'prefix': '10.1075'}\"\n}"
